<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Lobkowicz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lobkowicz family</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Lobkowicz is a Bohemian noble family recorded from the fourteenth century, raised to princely rank in 1624. Its members included the chancellor Zdeněk Vojtěch Popel of Lobkowicz and Prince Václav Eusebius, who acquired the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Sagan</a> in 1646. Prince Joseph Franz Maximilian was a patron of Beethoven. Its collections, confiscated under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a>, were largely restored after 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-lobkowicz.jpg" alt="House of Lobkowicz" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lobkowitz-fuerstliches-Wappen.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The first Lobkowiczs were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. Mikuláš Chudý, the Poor, of Újezd studied at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-university">Charles University</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and became a scribe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutna-hora">Kutná Hora</a>; King Wenceslaus IV gave him the village of Lobkovice, after which he styled himself, and made him supreme scribe of the kingdom in 1417. For capturing castles of rebellious nobles he was allowed to keep Hasištejn Castle. His sons were made free lords in 1459. Bohuslav Hasištejnský z Lobkovic was a humanist poet who collected more than 650 books, two thirds of which remain in the Lobkowicz Library.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Zdeněk Vojtěch and his wife Polyxena Pernštejn led the Catholic party at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> court, and after the Defenestration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1618 the Emperor's ministers took refuge in the Lobkowicz Palace. The Catholic victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> in 1620 restored the property the rebels had confiscated. Their son Václav Eusebius presided over the Imperial War Cabinet from 1652 and the Privy Council from 1669, and rebuilt Roudnice Castle as the family seat.</p><p>Joseph II created the 7th Prince Duke of Roudnice in 1786, and the family's immediacy over Störnstein was mediatised to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1807, which placed it among the mediatised houses of the high nobility. Maximilian Lobkowicz served democratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> as a diplomat and was ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> for the government in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. The family regained its property from the Nazis in 1945, lost it to the Communist government in 1948 and went into exile.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The princes built up a large collection of art and music. Ferdinand August added Veronese's David with the Head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goliath">Goliath</a> and Rubens's Hygieia and was the first to collect musical scores systematically. Ferdinand Philip brought back two paintings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> by Canaletto from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-willibald-gluck">Christoph Willibald Gluck</a> had accompanied him. Beethoven dedicated the 3rd, 5th and 6th symphonies to Joseph František Maximilian. The family has four main branches, of Roudnice, Křimice, Dolní Beřkovice and Mělník, and the Lobkowicz Palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague-castle">Prague Castle</a> and Nelahozeves Castle are museums.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lobkowicz family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1646 the <b>Lobkowicz family</b> held it, building a baroque palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-zagan">Duchy of Żagań</a></li></ul></section>
